# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-JapaneseFast-StudentText> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/Japanese-Basic-Course> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and API calls and its log of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that wrote the deck from the dossier's card table and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators). The research, the writing of the cards, these checks and the four reviews below were done by AI agents; no human review is recorded."@en ,
                 "Word list. A list of 122 words and phrases for a first Japanese course was drawn up by the AI agent from the library editor's brief, the same brief as the library's other basics decks (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; colours; family; greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns, adjectives and verbs), choosing the most basic, standard word for each concept. No frequency list, textbook word list or phrase-book list was copied: the two public-domain US Government courses below were read for the readings of numbers and months, the English meaning of the greetings already on the list and the facts in the notes."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every Japanese lexeme (language Q5287) whose lemma, in the tags ja, ja-hira, ja-kana or ja-hrkt, is a candidate front or one of its readings (468 rows). Words not found were looked up with the lexeme search (API calls 2): Wikidata has no Japanese lexeme for 六十, 八十 or ごめんなさい. The full JSON of the 205 lexemes found was fetched with API calls 3, and the lexeme chosen for each card (the numeral, noun, verb or adjective lexeme whose ja lemma is the front, or whose ja-hira lemma is the front for words written in kana; 120 cards) gave the hiragana reading of the front note, the sense, its glosses and its sense item (P5137). For the numbers 四, 七, 九 and 十七, whose second reading is a separate lexeme, both lexemes were kept (十四's second lexeme, じゅうし, L701027, was dropped with that reading in review 1). Eighteen more lexemes were fetched for the cards and notes (API calls 3b and 3c: お母さん, お父さん, お姉さん, 妹さん, お爺さん, お婆さん, 冷たい, 熱い, テーブル, ミルク, グレー, お早う, 有り難う, 良い, 〇, 初めまして, 始めまして and 居る (おる)), and in the review round the colour adjectives 赤い, 青い, 黒い, 白い, 黄色い and 茶色い (L33500–L33505), じゃ (L1158936, form じゃあ), 宜しく (L625296) and the honorific prefix お (L625391) (API calls 16–19). Query 7 looked for English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French senses linked to the chosen senses by translation statements (P5972), in either direction."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items linked from the senses were fetched with API calls 5 (labels and aliases in Japanese, English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish, French and mul); items for the greetings whose senses have none (good morning, good evening, good night, goodbye, please, thank you) and for 60 and 80 were found with the item search and two SPARQL queries (calls 6). 106 items in all give the English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French labels for numbers, days, months, colours, family members, everyday things and some greetings, the mul digit label of each number, and the Japanese label or alias checked against the front (六十 and 八十, which have no lexeme, come from the Japanese aliases of items Q79998 and Q712467)."@en ,
                 "Courses. Two public-domain US Government courses on the Internet Archive were read in the Archive's OCR text (calls 9 and 10): the Foreign Service Institute's Japanese FAST course (1988), whose Lesson 1 number table gives the readings of 0–99 (zero, shi/yon, shichi/nana, ku/kyuu, yon-juu, nana-juu, kyuu-juu), whose Lesson 20 and table of months give the months (shi-gatsu, shichi-gatsu, ku-gatsu), and whose lessons give the greetings with their English meaning and usage (Ohayoo gozaimasu, Konnichiwa, Konbanwa, Sayoonara, Oyasuminasai, Hajimemashite, arigatoo gozaimasu, Doo itashimashite, Sumimasen, onegai shimasu, hai, iie): three English back texts are its wording (good afternoon, you're welcome, excuse me, sorry), and it confirms the meaning of the others; and the Defense Language Institute's Japanese Basic Course (21 volumes, 1965–1970), which gives the days, the months, the colours with its grammar note on them, the sibling words with its note on talking about one's own and others' brothers and sisters (L-53, l. 84245–84252), and \"0) ZERO or REI\". The OCR of the Japanese script in both courses is unreadable, so the courses were used only for their romanised Japanese and English text, never for a spelling: every front and reading is spelled as in Wikidata. Three short phrases in notes are the kana spelling of FSI's romanised text (じゃあ、また for \"Jaa, mata\", どうぞよろしく for \"Doozo yoroshiku\", これ、お願いします for \"kore onegai shimasu\"); their words are in Wikidata (じゃあ as a form of L1158936, どうぞ L984458, 宜しく L625296 with ja-hira よろしく, お願いします the card's lexeme). The text was searched with a script (call 11) and every passage cited in the evidence was read in the OCR text around it. The War Department's Japanese phrase books TM 30-611 (1944) and TM 30-341 (1943) were found and downloaded; TM 30-611 was consulted only to confirm 灰色 (\"Haiiro\"), and nothing was taken from either."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the Japanese word as it is usually written: in kanji where Japanese normally writes kanji, with standard okurigana (大きい, 新しい, 食べる), and in kana where Japanese normally writes kana (the greetings, いい, ある, いる, する, パン). For the greetings the ja-hira lemma of the lexeme was chosen over its kanji lemma (こんにちは, not 今日は; ありがとうございます, not 有難うございます), and お願いします keeps its usual kanji. Numbers are written in kanji numerals (零, 一 … 十, 十一 … 二十, 三十 … 九十, 百) and the months in kanji (一月 … 十二月); the days are 月曜日 … 日曜日. Every front with kanji has a front note, tagged ja, with its reading in hiragana from the lexeme's ja-hira lemma; where a number has two common readings both are given, with the reading the FSI course uses in the tens first (よん、し; なな、しち; きゅう、く; じゅうなな、じゅうしち); for 十四 and 十九 only じゅうよん and じゅうきゅう are given, since じゅうし and じゅうく, which the FSI table also lists, are not the usual readings when counting (review 1); and for 0 the reading れい, with ゼロ in the note. There is no romanisation anywhere on the cards. Card ids are lower-case Hepburn without macrons."@en ,
                 "The back is the meaning in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, one text per language, or for a number its digits, tagged zxx (text in no language) and taken from the item's mul label. Each meaning follows the item's label or an alias in that language where the item has one and it is the everyday word, or a gloss or translation in Wikidata. Counted part by part (the comma-separated parts of a text): of the 558 meanings, 387 come wholly from Wikidata (en 77, sv 58, ko 57, de 68, es 62, fr 65 of 93 cards); 3 English ones are the FSI course's English, wholly or with a Wikidata label (you're welcome, excuse me, sorry; hello, good afternoon); 13 are mixed, one part from Wikidata and another the authors' own (for example こんにちは: hej from Wikidata, god dag own; 寝る: to sleep from Wikidata, to go to bed own); and 155 are wholly the authors' own renderings, written with the help of AI: mostly the verbs and adjectives, whose lexemes have English glosses but no items, the greetings, whose items lack most languages, and Korean adjectives in their attributive form (큰, 작은), as the library's other basics decks give them. Where Japanese makes a distinction the other language does not, both words are given (兄: 형 또는 오빠; 祖父: farfar eller morfar), and where a meaning needs narrowing it is given in brackets (古い: old (of things); ある and いる: of things, of people and animals). Verbs are in the infinitive (English \"to …\", Swedish \"att …\"), Korean verbs in the dictionary form. Every text is tagged with its language: the front ja, the front note ja, the back en, sv, ko, de, es and fr, digits zxx."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note, in the six back languages, was added where it helps a beginner and a content source gives its fact: the second reading of 四, 七 and 九 with an example each, ゼロ and 〇 for 0, the Arabic numerals of the months (1月) and the readings しがつ, しちがつ and くがつ, the -い adjective of five colours (赤い, 青い, 黄色い, 黒い, 白い: the DLI course and Wikidata), 緑の for green, which has none, and for brown both 茶色の and 茶色い (the DLI course gives only the noun; Wikidata L33505 has the adjective), グレー, the polite forms お母さん, お父さん, おじいさん and おばあさん (Wikidata: the honorific prefix お and the lexemes of the forms), and for 兄, 姉 and 妹 the words for someone else's (お兄さん, お姉さん: the DLI course's note on siblings; 妹さん: Wikidata's gloss \"of somebody else\"), the pronunciation of the final は of こんにちは and こんばんは, the casual おはよう and ありがとう, when おやすみなさい and さようなら are said, どうぞよろしく after はじめまして, すみません as a thank-you, how お願いします is used, はい as the answer to a roll call, and among the nouns, adjectives and verbs: 家 also family, ミルク, 本 as a counter, テーブル, the kana spellings いす, イヌ and ネコ, 町 as a unit of local government, read まち or ちょう, 男の人 and 女の人, 子ども and 友だち, 金, 夜 also evening, 良い, 冷たい and 熱い, ある and いる, the reading ゆく of 行く, and 寝る also meaning to lie down. Notes are our own wording, and each note's fact comes from Wikidata (lexemes, glosses, item labels and aliases) or from one of the two courses, as the dossier's per-card evidence cites; the one exception is a fact about Swedish, not Japanese (a Swedish request usually ends with tack), the authors' own. The notes were not checked against English Wiktionary."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every front was looked up in English Wiktionary (calls 12: its wikitext through the API, read with Python): all 122 pages have a Japanese section, and every reading in a front note appears in it. Only fronts and readings were checked there; no meaning or note was checked against Wiktionary. Wiktionary is CC BY-SA and was used to verify facts only: no definition, example or note was taken from it. JMdict was not consulted."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder: for every card that cites Wikidata (all 122), the front against the main lexeme's lemma (120 checks), each front-note reading against the hiragana lemmas of the card's lexemes (108), that the cited sense still exists (120), each back text against a label or alias of a linked item in its language (396), each number's digits against the item's mul label (29) and the front against the item's Japanese label or alias (76): 849 checks on 129 lexemes and 106 items, all passing in the final run after the review round. Where an item's label does not match the card, no check was added and the difference is listed in the dossier (98 such differences, all where the card's text is not taken from that item, such as the many items with no Japanese label in kanji or no label in a back language)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: ごめんなさい, which has no Wikidata lexeme and is in neither course (replaced by はじめまして); the polite family words (お母さん …), the -い adjectives of the colours, テーブル and ゼロ, which appear only in notes; a note on 弟, 息子 and 娘 for someone else's family (弟さん, 息子さん, 娘さん), since no CC0 or public-domain source found gives those forms (Wikidata has no lexeme for them, calls 2); 日 and 年, whose readings depend on context; the pronouns 私 and あなた, whose use needs more than a flashcard; Arabic-numeral months (in notes only); and the War Department phrase books and the Bloch–Jorden Spoken Japanese (1945), the latter because its copyright status was not established."@en ,
                 "Selection: 122 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100 (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (赤, 青, 緑, 黄色, 黒, 白, 灰色, 茶色); ten family members, since Japanese has no everyday word for a brother or sister of unspecified age (母, 父, 兄, 弟, 姉, 妹, 息子, 娘, 祖父, 祖母), in the plain form used of one's own family; twelve greetings and polite phrases (こんにちは, おはようございます, こんばんは, おやすみなさい, さようなら, はじめまして, ありがとうございます, どういたしまして, すみません, お願いします, はい, いいえ); 20 everyday nouns (家, 水, パン, 牛乳, 本, 机, 椅子, 窓, 犬, 猫, 町, 学校, 男, 女, 子供, 友達, お金, 仕事, 手, 夜); eight adjectives (大きい, 小さい, いい, 悪い, 新しい, 古い, 寒い, 暑い); and 16 verbs in the dictionary form (ある, いる, 行く, 来る, する, 話す, 食べる, 飲む, 住む, 読む, 書く, 寝る, 働く, 買う, 見る, 言う). The colours are the nouns, so that all eight are the same kind of word (the DLI course notes that only red, blue, yellow, black and white are -い adjectives); the five that the DLI course lists as -い adjectives give that adjective in a note, and 茶色, for which Wikidata also has 茶色い, gives both forms. The list was drawn up by the AI agent from the brief for a first course, not copied from any frequency list, textbook or phrase book. Every word has a Wikidata lexeme except 六十 and 八十 (Wikidata items and the FSI course). The deck is studied front to back only: some backs nearly repeat in a language (Spanish buenas noches for こんばんは and おやすみなさい; Korean 있다 for ある and いる; 안녕하세요 for three greetings), so the reverse direction would be ambiguous."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from three sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: the Japanese lemmas and readings, glosses, translations and labels), the Foreign Service Institute's Japanese FAST course (1988) and the Defense Language Institute's Japanese Basic Course (1965–1970), both works of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (the readings of numbers and months, three English back texts of greetings, the romanised phrases spelled in kana in three notes, and a few facts of usage in the notes). Section 105 applies in the United States; what the deck takes from these two works is readings, the English meaning of standard greetings and facts of usage, which are not protectable expression anywhere, so the deck's CC0 dedication does not depend on § 105 applying outside the US. The Defense Language Institute's notice that its pamphlets are \"NOT for general distribution\" and not to be \"released to other persons, quoted or extracted for publication\", and its preface's request that \"requests for authorization to reproduce\" go to its Director, are internal distribution instructions, not a copyright. The selection of words is the authors' own. The meanings not taken from Wikidata are the authors' own renderings, written with the help of AI; single words and fixed greetings such as \"good night\" or \"god natt\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression. English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to verify facts only: no definition, example, note or list was taken from it, and every fact in a note has a CC0 or public-domain content source. So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 122 cards: every Japanese front, reading, meaning and note against the content sources (Wikidata, the FSI course, the DLI course); every front and reading against English Wiktionary (its meanings and notes were not compared); the licences and licence evidence of the four sources; a live re-run of the builder's Wikidata checks; the library checker (Solid Memo's SHACL shapes, DCAT-AP and the library rules). Done by an AI agent and by machine; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has evidence from a content source (Wikidata for 122 cards, the FSI or DLI course for the readings of numbers and months, the greetings and the facts in the notes), and English Wiktionary's Japanese entries contain every front and every reading. The re-run of the builder's Wikidata checks covered all 122 cards that cite Wikidata: 859 checks on 130 lexemes and 108 items (fronts, readings, senses, back labels and aliases, digits, Japanese labels); they all passed. The library checker found the first draft's cards, written in card format 4 like the other basics decks, invalid, since format 4 asks every note to have an English text and the reading notes are Japanese only; the cards were written in card format 5, which allows a note in any language, and the deck is valid. The four licences were confirmed against the evidence quoted in each source's record. Discrepancies between the sources and how they were resolved are listed in the dossier. (The figures are those of this round; after reviews 1–3 the checks are 849 on 129 lexemes and 106 items.)"@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 122 cards and their dossier entries: a live re-fetch of every lexeme and item the cards and notes cite, every front, reading, number, day and month, the cited senses and their glosses, the labels and aliases behind every back in the six languages, a re-run of the builder's Wikidata checks, the count of meanings taken from Wikidata, and every FSI and DLI line cited for a note, read in the OCR text; English Wiktionary for 十九 and 茶色い. Done by an AI agent and by machine; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every front, reading, number, day and month was found right and every cited course line quoted accurately. Two errors: the Spanish notes of 父 and 兄 wrote \"de el\" for \"del\", and the Spanish note of 町 called the municipality a «ciudad», where Wikidata's Q1059478 has «pueblo». Two warnings: the record said brown has no -い adjective, while Wikidata has 茶色い (L33505); and 町 cited the administrative sense (S1) for its main meaning, and tagged Korean 동네 as Wikidata. Seven suggestions: ちょう as the reading of the administrative 町; tack rather than snälla for お願いします; varm, het for 暑い; じゅうし and じゅうく are not the usual readings when counting; テーブル is any table, not one for eating at; 男 and 子供 were linked to items that are not the source of their backs (Q6581097 male, Q29514218 offspring); and 弟 lacked the family note. All were checked against live Wikidata and the courses. The two errors and two warnings were fixed (del; «pueblo»; 茶色い in the note, citing L33505, and the record corrected; 町 now cites S2 with Q3957, and 동네 is counted as the authors' own). Six suggestions were applied (the reading まち or ちょう; vänligen with a note that a Swedish request ends with tack; varm, het; じゅうし and じゅうく dropped from the 十四 and 十九 notes; the テーブル note; Q6581097 and Q29514218 removed from the evidence). The note for 弟 was declined: no CC0 or public-domain source gives 弟さん, and the DLI passage on -san (l. 6276–6283) concerns names; the same reasoning now governs the other family notes (see review 3)."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every line of the deck: the 122 fronts and front notes, the 558 non-digit backs and 29 digit backs, all back notes in the six languages, the titles, descriptions and keywords, the study direction and the record; mechanically, every language tag, the hiragana-only front notes, the absence of Latin script on the fronts and of a reading note on kana-only fronts; by hand, every Korean particle after Japanese text; live Wikidata labels of the colour items and of Q1059478; conventions compared with the template decks' latest versions. Done by an AI agent and by machine; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every language tag, front note and Korean particle was found right. Two errors, the same as review 1's: Spanish \"de el\" in the notes of 父 and 兄, and «ciudad» for the 町 municipality. Two warnings: the Korean colour backs mixed 빨강, 노랑, 검정, 하양 and 초록 with 파란색, 회색 and 갈색; and snälla, colloquial, for the polite お願いします. Ten suggestions: family notes for 弟, 息子 and 娘; 茶色い in the brown note; Swedish noun forms or German adjectives for the colours; classificateur rather than spécificatif in the French note on 本; a stronger note on さようなら; sehen, schauen for 見る; \"sich bemüht hat\" in the German note on すみません; \"für die eines anderen\" in the German family notes; why 男の人 and 女の人 are used; and the description's account of where meanings come from. Fixed: both errors; the Korean colours are now 빨간색, 파란색, 초록색, 녹색 (green), 노란색, 검은색, 흰색, 회색, 갈색, as in the template decks (빨간색, 녹색, 노란색, 검은색 and 흰색 are Wikidata aliases, 초록색 the authors' own); お願いします is vänligen, with a Swedish note that a request usually ends with tack; and the suggestions on 茶色い, classificateur, sehen, schauen, the German wording of the すみません and family notes, and the description (now naming item labels, lexeme glosses and translations, in all six languages) were applied. Declined: notes for 弟, 息子 and 娘, which no CC0 or public-domain source supports; the colour forms, since German nouns match the Japanese nouns and Wikidata's German labels, and Swedish names a colour by its common adjective form, Wikidata's Swedish label; the さようなら and 男の人 notes, since the FSI course says only that さようなら implies not meeting again that day and no source gives the reason for 男の人, so the notes keep to what the sources say; and writing 위키데이터 in the Korean description, since the template's Korean description also writes Wikidata."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole deck and dossier against the template decks and the library's rules (docs/deck-library.md, the topics and external vocabularies): a byte-for-byte rebuild from the builder, a live re-run of the Wikidata checks, the library checker, the licence evidence of every source checked live (Wikidata:Licensing, the Wiktionary footer, the Internet Archive metadata), the courses' title pages, prefaces and notices in the OCR text, every cited course passage, the provenance tag of every back text checked against live Wikidata, every lexeme and item cited for a note, card ids, metadata, attribution and every statement about review. Done by an AI agent and by machine; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The CC0 decision, the attribution comments, the creator, publisher and release links, the metadata, the themes and languages and the card ids were found right, and no statement claims a human review. One error: the record claimed checks against English Wiktionary that were never run (the English meanings and the notes; only fronts and readings were checked). Five warnings: the own-versus-others rule in the notes of 母, 父, 祖父 and 祖母 had no CC0 or public-domain source and the evidence cited a section that did not exist; the FSI course was credited with the greetings' English though no back was tagged as FSI, while three are its wording; several backs counted as Wikidata were only partly from it; the record said brown has no -い adjective; and \"every spelling on the cards comes from Wikidata\" overlooked three kana phrases in notes taken from FSI's romanised text. Seven suggestions: TM 30-611's use for 灰色; the DLI notice quoted in full; the Wikidata source comment to name glosses and the translation; dossier §6 and §9 to agree on 町; the line numbers for すみません and support for \"usually ゼロ\"; \"drawn up by hand\"; and card format 5 instead of the template's 4. Fixed: the Wiktionary claims were cut from the compilation, review 0, the Wiktionary source record and the dossier; the notes of 母, 父, 祖父 and 祖母 now give only the polite form (お母さん …), which Wikidata supports through the honorific prefix お (L625391) and the forms' lexemes, while 兄 and 姉 cite the DLI note (l. 84245–84252) and 妹 its Wikidata gloss; each back text is now tagged part by part as Wikidata, FSI, mixed or the authors' own, and the counts in the record follow; the 茶色い and spelling statements were corrected and L33500–L33505, L1158936 and L625296 cited; TM 30-611, the DLI notice, the Wikidata comment, 町, the すみません lines (l. 2639–2643) and \"drawn up by the AI agent\" were fixed, and the 0 note now says only \"also ゼロ\". Card format 5 was kept, as recorded in the dossier, since format 4 requires an English text in every note and the reading notes are Japanese. The cards' dcterms:created and the compilation's end time were also set to 12:00, as the template decks write them."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Japanese lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\" Content source: the Japanese lemmas and their hiragana readings, the lexemes behind the notes, the English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French labels and aliases of the items linked from the senses, the lexeme glosses behind some meanings (the English of most verbs and of 悪い and 暑い; the Korean and German of 食べる and 飲む) and one P5972 translation (the English of 寒い: L3296)."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Fsi-JapaneseFast-StudentText>
    dcterms:title "Japanese: Familiarization & Short-term Training (FAST), Student Text (Foreign Service Institute, 1988), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "The Japanese Section, School of Language Studies, Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State (Tomoko Tanaka Campen and the FSI Japanese instructors; final version edited by Cornelius C. Kubler)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"JAPANESE Familiarization & Short-term Training 1988 Japanese Section School of Language Studies FOREIGN SERVICE INSTITUTE U. S. DEPARTMENT OF STATE\", and the preface says the course \"is one of a series of similar foreign language courses produced at the Foreign Service Institute that are intended to train U.S. Government personnel assigned to overseas posts\" and that it was revised by \"Mrs. Tomoko Tanaka Campen, Senior Instructor in the FSI Japanese Section ... along with the team of instructors that she heads\". 17 U.S.C. § 105 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/105): \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". The Internet Archive item's metadata (https://archive.org/metadata/Fsi-JapaneseFast-StudentText) gives licenseurl \"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/publicdomain/\". Recorded here with the Public Domain Mark: free of known copyright. Content source: the readings of the numbers and months (confirming those of the lexemes), three English back texts (good afternoon for こんにちは, you're welcome, excuse me, sorry), the confirmation of the meaning of the other greetings, the romanised phrases whose kana spelling is in three notes (Jaa, mata; Doozo yoroshiku; kore onegai shimasu), and facts of usage in the notes."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Japanese-Basic-Course>
    dcterms:title "Japanese: Basic Course, volumes 1–21 (Defense Language Institute, 1965–1970), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "Defense Language Institute, U.S. Department of Defense" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "A work of the United States Government: volume 1 reads \"DEFENSE LANGUAGE INSTITUTE JAPANESE SOUNDS OF JAPANESE SPEECH BASIC COURSE VOLUME I ... DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE ... April 1967\", and its preface says \"The Japanese Course, consisting of 160 lessons in 21 volumes, is one of the Defense Language Institute's Basic Course Series. The material was developed at the Defense Language Institute\". 17 U.S.C. § 105 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/105): \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". The notice in full (its two OCR columns reassembled): \"This pamphlet is for use by the faculty and staff of the Defense Language Institute solely for official purposes. It is NOT for general distribution. It may NOT be released to other persons, quoted or extracted for publication, or otherwise copied or distributed without specific permission in each instance from the Director, Defense Language Institute.\" The preface adds: \"All inquiries concerning these materials, including requests for authorization to reproduce, will be addressed to the Director, Defense Language Institute\". Both are internal distribution instructions of a government agency, not a claim of copyright, which 17 U.S.C. § 105 rules out for the agency's own work; the Internet Archive publishes the course openly (metadata https://archive.org/metadata/Japanese-Basic-Course, licenseurl \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/\"). Recorded here with the Public Domain Mark: free of known copyright. Content source: confirmation of the colours, family words, days and months with their English meaning, and facts of usage in the notes (the -い colour adjectives and 緑の/茶色の, the sibling words for one's own and others' family, 0 as ZERO or REI, はじめまして)."@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Japanese entries)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/犬 — page footer: \"Definitions and other text are available under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike License; additional terms may apply.\", linking to https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/. Used to verify facts only: every front was looked up for a Japanese entry containing its reading; the meanings and notes were not checked against it, and nothing was taken from it."@en .

